// PA-v0.21-004 (#1280): Barrier intrinsics — mfence / sfence / lfence / pause.
//
// Trait BarrierOps declares the four architectural memory-order fence
// intrinsics as first-class stdlib primitives, so barrier discipline is
// visible at the callsite (`barrier_full()` reads clearly) rather than
// hidden inside an `unsafe { mfence; }` block.
//
// Consumed by paideia-os R18-M5-002 (#776) — the TLB-shootdown mfence
// audit — which currently emits mfence via raw asm; once #1280 lands
// downstream, tlb_shootdown.pdx can migrate to `barrier_full()`.
//
// Semantics (Intel SDM Vol 3A §8.2 + §11.10):
//   barrier_full()   → MFENCE: fully-serializing memory fence. Every load
//                      and every store before the fence completes globally
//                      before any load or store after the fence begins.
//                      Use when a store must be visible to another CPU
//                      before a subsequent load on THIS CPU observes what
//                      the other CPU wrote in response — the classic
//                      Dekker pattern. Also required to order plain WB
//                      stores before UC MMIO stores from the same CPU.
//   barrier_store()  → SFENCE: store-store fence. Every store before the
//                      fence completes globally before any store after
//                      the fence begins. Cheaper than mfence when only
//                      store ordering matters. Common in ring-buffer
//                      producer paths: write the payload, sfence, publish
//                      the index. On x86 TSO, plain WB→WB stores are
//                      already ordered, so sfence is primarily useful when
//                      one side of the ordering is a WC store (movnt* /
//                      NT stores).
//   barrier_load()   → LFENCE: load-load fence + speculation barrier.
//                      Every load before the fence completes before any
//                      load after the fence begins. On modern (post-2018)
//                      Intel/AMD, LFENCE is also a full speculation
//                      barrier — the pipeline drains before subsequent
//                      instructions may issue. Used for Spectre v1
//                      mitigation on data-dependent branches, and to
//                      pair with SFENCE on the consumer side of a ring
//                      buffer that read the published index and needs to
//                      order the subsequent payload load after.
//   cpu_pause()      → PAUSE (F3 90): spinloop hint. NOT a fence. Signals
//                      to the hardware that this loop iteration is a
//                      busy-wait, letting the pipeline back off (SMT
//                      partner runs faster; power draw drops). Used
//                      inside every spin-wait: while (locked) { pause; }.
//
// Effect discipline:
//   barrier_full / barrier_store / barrier_load are !{Atomic} — they are
//   the ordering primitives that make Atomic-effect sequences visible in
//   a defined order across cores.
//   cpu_pause is !{} — architecturally a NOP hint; caller sits in an
//   Atomic context already (the spin-wait it decorates).
//
// Capability: none. All four are unprivileged; usable anywhere.
//
// Visibility discipline: exposed OUTSIDE `unsafe { }` blocks intentionally.
// Barrier discipline is a code-review-visible property of correct
// concurrent code — every one of these calls signals to a reviewer that
// the writer thought about memory ordering. Hiding them in raw-asm blocks
// inside kernel .pdx files (the current situation before #1280) is
// deliberately being reversed.

trait BarrierOps {
  fn barrier_full() -> () !{Atomic};
  fn barrier_store() -> () !{Atomic};
  fn barrier_load() -> () !{Atomic};
  fn cpu_pause() -> ();
}
